%I A398139 #22 Aug 06 2026 08:42:24
%S A398139 1,2,3,4,5,8,14,20,48,54,98,350,440
%N A398139 Numbers m such that the distinct prime factors of the product m*(m+1)*(m+2) are consecutive primes beginning with 2 (that is, the product is a term of A055932).
%C A398139 Call a positive integer "prime-complete" if all its distinct prime factors are consecutive primes beginning with 2. This sequence logically follows sequence A141399 of numbers, w, with prime-complete products w*(w+1).
%C A398139 Let M_3(m) = m*(m+1)*(m+2) and define w = m*(m+2). Then w + 1 = (m+1)^2 and w*(w+1) = (m+1)*M_3(m). Since m+1 already divides M_3(m), multiplication by m+1 introduces no new distinct prime factors. Therefore rad(M_3(m)) = rad(w*(w+1)).
%C A398139 Consequently, m is a term of this sequence iff m*(m+2) is a term of A141399. Thus this sequence is the inverse image, of A141399 under the map m -> m*(m+2); its terms are obtained from those terms w of A141399 for which w+1 is a square, by setting m = sqrt(w+1) - 1.
%C A398139 This relationship is one-way with respect to completeness. If the displayed terms of A141399 form its complete sequence, then the displayed terms here also form a complete sequence, with final term 440. However, an independent proof that 440 is the final term of this sequence would exclude only later terms w of A141399 for which w+1 is a square. It would not exclude later terms of A141399 outside that quadratic image.
%C A398139 The products, M_3, match terms of sequence A385415.
%C A398139 Conjecture: There are no more terms in this sequence past 440. This conjecture is based on analysis of the growth rate of the minimum prime index values of the GPF of M_3(m) (call it iota_min(M_3(m))) in primorial intervals, such that P_r <= M_3(m) < P_(r+1). For intervals r=2 through r=22 the iota_min progression is: 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 5, 6, 10, 8, 10, 13, 13, 16, 16, 20, 22, 26, 24, 30, 31, 39. The significance of iota_min in these intervals is that the maximum value of omega for M_3(m) in these intervals is r, so if iota_min exceeds r, it is impossible for there to be a prime-complete product in the interval. In the sequence above, iota_min exceeds r for r=14 and above. The links to the program and output data for this analysis are below.
%H A398139 Ken Clements, <a href="https://github.com/kenatiod/Delta_min/blob/main/Delta_min_N3/Primorial_Intervals_N3.py">Program to calculate iota_min in primorial intervals</a>.
%H A398139 Ken Clements, <a href="https://github.com/kenatiod/Delta_min/blob/main/Delta_min_N3/Primorial_Intervals_N3_Output.txt">Output of iota_min program</a>.
%F A398139 For m>=1, a(n) = m for the next n, if m*(m+2) is in A141399.
%e A398139 a(6) = 8 because 8*(8+2) = 80, and 80 is a term of A141399. Correspondingly, 8*9*10 = 720, which is A385415(6).
%e A398139 a(13) = 440 because 440*(440+2) = 194480, and 194480 is a term of A141399. Correspondingly, 440*441*442 = 85765680, which is A385415(13).
%e A398139 The number 6 is not a term because 6*(6+2) = 48 is not a term of A141399; that is, 6*7*8 = 336 = 2^4 * 3 * 7 has 7 as a prime factor but does not have 5 as a prime factor.
%t A398139 Select[Range[2^16], Or[IntegerQ@ Log2[#], And[EvenQ[#], Union@ Differences@ PrimePi@ FactorInteger[#][[All, 1]] == {1}]] &[#*(# + 1)*(# + 2)] &] (* _Michael De Vlieger_, Jul 30 2026 *)
%o A398139 (Python)
%o A398139 from sympy import prime, primefactors
%o A398139 def is_prime_complete(n):
%o A398139     factors = primefactors(n)
%o A398139     return factors[-1] == prime(len(factors))
%o A398139 def is_A141399_term(w):
%o A398139     return is_prime_complete(w * (w + 1))
%o A398139 def terms(limit):
%o A398139     return [m for m in range(1, limit + 1) if is_A141399_term(m * (m + 2))]
%o A398139 print(terms(500))
%o A398139 (PARI) isok(m) = my(f=factor(m*(m+1)*(m+2))[, 1]~); f==primes(#f); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Jul 23 2026
%Y A398139 Cf. A141399, A385415, A055932, A007947, A007531, A398140.
%K A398139 nonn,more,new
%O A398139 1,2
